<TeXmacs|2.1.4>

<style|article>

<\body>
  <\theorem>
    Among six people, there are always three people who mutually know each
    other or three people who mutually do not know each other.
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    Let <math|P> be an arbitrary person among the six. Then either one of
    the following cases holds:

    <\description>
      <item*|Case 1><math|P> knows at least three other persons, which may
      be called <math|Q>, <math|R> and <math|S>. If two of them know each
      other, then they form, together with <math|P>, three people who
      mutually know each other. Otherwise, <math|Q>, <math|R> and <math|S>
      are three people who mutually do not know each other.

      <item*|Case 2><math|P> does not know at least three other persons,
      which may be called <math|Q>, <math|R> and <math|S>. If two of them do
      not know each other, then they form, together with <math|P>, three
      people who mutually do not know each other. Otherwise, <math|Q>,
      <math|R> and <math|S> are three people who mutually know each other.
    </description>

    Since <math|P> knows or does not know each of the five other persons,
    one of the two cases always holds.
  </proof>
</body>

